Let $F$ be a field of characteristic $p$ and put $K=F(t)$. Suppose $u\in F^{1\times m}$, $v\in F^{m\times 1}$, and strictly upper triangular $B_1,\ldots,B_n\in\mathop{\mathrm{Mat}}_m(F)$ represent the coefficients of a polynomial $f$ as in <a href="#eq:coefficient-representation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:coefficient-representation">[eq:coefficient-representation]</a>. With the word series from Section <a href="#sec:words" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:words">2</a>, define $$\label{eq:fundamental-series-matrix}
 H(z)=\sum_w B_w h_w(z).$$ Then $H\in\mathop{\mathrm{GL}}_m(K[[z]])$, $H(0)=I_m$, and $$\label{eq:formula-shift-system}
 \begin{gathered}
 h_f=uHv,\qquad \sigma(H)=A(z)H,\\
 A(z)=I_m+\sum_{i=1}^n b_i(z)B_i=\frac{P(z)}{Q(z)},
 \end{gathered}$$ where $$\label{eq:system-denominator}
 \begin{gathered}
 Q(z)=\prod_{i=1}^n(1-a_i z),\qquad Q(0)=1,\\
 \deg_z Q\leq n,\qquad \deg_z P_{ab}\leq n
 \quad(1\leq a,b\leq m).
 \end{gathered}$$ Here $P\in\mathop{\mathrm{Mat}}_m(K[z])$, and $a_i=(1+t)^i$ and $b_i=z/(1-a_i z)$ are as in Section <a href="#sec:words" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:words">2</a>. If $f\neq 0$, then $uHv\neq 0$.

hypotheses.statement:
Let $F$ have characteristic zero, and let $0\ne f\in F\langle x_1,\ldots,x_n\rangle$ have a formula of size at most $s$. There are $m\le2s$, a nonzero constant row $u$, a constant column $v$, and an invertible matrix $G\in F[[z]]^{m\times m}$ such that $$\label{eq:formula-system}
 G'=AG,\qquad G(0)=I_m,\qquad
 A(z)=\frac{P(z)}{q(z)},\qquad
 q(z)=\prod_{i=1}^n(z+i),\quad \deg P\le n-1,$$ where $P$ is a polynomial matrix. Moreover, $$\label{eq:formula-output}
 h_f(z)=\sum_w f_wh_w(z)=uG(z)v\ne0.$$

Let $F$ be a field of characteristic zero and let $V\subset F[[z]]$ be a vector space of finite positive dimension $k$ over $F$. There is a basis $b_1,\ldots,b_k$ of $V$ with distinct orders $0\le a_1<\cdots<a_k$. For this basis, $$\label{eq:wronskian-order}
 \operatorname{ord}_0 W(b_1,\ldots,b_k)
 =\sum_{\ell=1}^k a_\ell-\binom{k}{2}.$$ In particular, the Wronskian of any basis of $V$ is nonzero, its order does not depend on the basis, and every nonzero member of $V$ has order at most $a_k$.

